1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,143
Coach: When asked about an experience,

2
00:00:02,143 --> 00:00:06,857
Coach: you do not need to begin with your most impressive achievement.

3
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:10,715
Coach: Choose something specific that lets you explain what happened,

4
00:00:10,715 --> 00:00:15,429
Coach: what you did and what you can honestly say about it.

5
00:00:15,779 --> 00:00:20,493
Coach: We will use a fictional worked example, not anyone’s personal record.

6
00:00:20,493 --> 00:00:26,065
Coach: Three volunteers prepared a one-page visitor guide for a community open afternoon.

7
00:00:26,065 --> 00:00:29,922
Coach: The participant drew the map, Maya listed session times,

8
00:00:29,922 --> 00:00:32,065
Coach: and Noah assembled the guide.

9
00:00:32,415 --> 00:00:35,843
You: I drew the map for a visitor guide.

10
00:00:35,843 --> 00:00:37,986
You: The day before the event,

11
00:00:37,986 --> 00:00:43,986
You: I noticed that my map said Studio B while Maya’s schedule said Blue Room.

12
00:00:44,336 --> 00:00:49,907
Coach: This gives us an episode to examine: conflicting labels while preparing a guide.

13
00:00:49,907 --> 00:00:54,622
Coach: It is more specific than saying I helped at an event,

14
00:00:54,622 --> 00:00:58,479
Coach: and it does not yet claim a dramatic result.

15
00:00:58,829 --> 00:01:03,972
Coach: The participant did not know whether the names meant the same room.

16
00:01:03,972 --> 00:01:06,972
Coach: Instead of guessing, they asked the coordinator.

17
00:01:06,972 --> 00:01:11,686
Coach: The coordinator confirmed that both names referred to the same room

18
00:01:11,686 --> 00:01:15,115
Coach: and that its door sign said Blue Room.

19
00:01:15,465 --> 00:01:20,179
Coach: The participant changed the map label and told Maya and Noah.

20
00:01:20,179 --> 00:01:24,036
Coach: Maya checked the schedule; Noah assembled the updated guide.

21
00:01:24,036 --> 00:01:27,465
Coach: That is the shared work surrounding the episode,

22
00:01:27,465 --> 00:01:30,465
Coach: not evidence that one person completed everything.

23
00:01:30,815 --> 00:01:36,386
Coach: Privately identify the part of this experience you could explain in more detail.

24
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:40,672
Coach: Which moment involves an uncertainty, an action or a decision?

25
00:01:40,672 --> 00:01:45,815
Coach: You can use the supplied case without pretending it happened to you.

26
00:01:54,165 --> 00:01:58,879
Coach: A specific account does not need every detail of the afternoon.

27
00:01:58,879 --> 00:02:04,451
Coach: Include enough context for the listener to understand the problem and your part.

28
00:02:04,451 --> 00:02:10,451
Coach: Extra details are useful only when they help explain the point you are making.

29
00:02:10,801 --> 00:02:13,372
Coach: The source also limits the result:

30
00:02:13,372 --> 00:02:18,515
Coach: the participant saw one visitor use the map to find Blue Room.

31
00:02:18,515 --> 00:02:21,516
Coach: There was no survey or total count,

32
00:02:21,516 --> 00:02:26,230
Coach: and no comparison showing what would have happened without the change.

33
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:30,009
Coach: Those limits do not make the experience unusable.

34
00:02:30,009 --> 00:02:34,294
Coach: The label check is still something the participant can explain.

35
00:02:34,294 --> 00:02:40,294
Coach: Do not add a larger outcome simply to make the story sound more impressive.

36
00:02:40,644 --> 00:02:45,358
Coach: For your own practice, consider an ordinary experience you remember well.

37
00:02:45,358 --> 00:02:50,930
Coach: It could involve preparing something, helping with a task or noticing a problem.

38
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:54,359
Coach: Choose an episode small enough to describe clearly,

39
00:02:54,359 --> 00:02:59,073
Coach: with a part that you can explain from your own experience.

40
00:02:59,423 --> 00:03:02,852
Coach: If one topic feels too personal, choose another.

41
00:03:02,852 --> 00:03:07,137
Coach: You can also use the fictional case for language practice,

42
00:03:07,137 --> 00:03:10,137
Coach: while keeping it labelled as an example.

43
00:03:10,137 --> 00:03:13,566
Coach: A useful episode does not need an award,

44
00:03:13,566 --> 00:03:16,566
Coach: a leadership title or a dramatic ending.

45
00:03:16,916 --> 00:03:21,630
Coach: Privately choose one episode and name the part you could explain.

46
00:03:21,630 --> 00:03:23,773
Coach: Keep the choice to yourself.

47
00:03:23,773 --> 00:03:27,630
Coach: What was happening, and where did you take part?

48
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:31,916
Coach: Pause here if you would like more time to think.

49
00:03:40,266 --> 00:03:44,980
Coach: A useful starting point is an experience with a clear setting

50
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,552
Coach: and an action you can describe.

51
00:03:47,552 --> 00:03:50,980
Coach: It need not prove an entire personal quality.

52
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:56,552
Coach: In the next section, we will separate your contribution from the team’s work.
